WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.840
Imagine for a second that you've just been handed the ultimate engineering contract.

00:05.140 --> 00:07.580
You have unlimited funding, you have unlimited time,

00:08.120 --> 00:11.720
and your assignment is to build the most complex machine in the known universe.

00:11.940 --> 00:14.360
Which is the human neocortex, right?

00:14.360 --> 00:15.740
Exactly, the human neocortex.

00:15.960 --> 00:18.300
So how do you actually sit down and do it?

00:18.320 --> 00:23.140
If you approach this like a traditional architect or a structural engineer,

00:23.920 --> 00:27.320
your very first instinct is going to be writing out a master blueprint.

00:27.520 --> 00:28.160
Oh, absolutely.

00:28.160 --> 00:29.540
Right. You want a schematic.

00:29.540 --> 00:34.960
You want this massive document detailing exactly where all 16 billion neurons belong,

00:35.180 --> 00:39.040
how every single one of those trillions of synaptic connections is routed,

00:39.220 --> 00:45.320
and what specific computational task each cell is supposed to execute.

00:45.460 --> 00:47.460
Yeah, and it is the most intuitive approach in the world

00:47.460 --> 00:51.060
because it mirrors how human beings build literally everything.

00:51.180 --> 00:53.760
I mean, we construct skyscrapers from CAD drawings, right?

00:53.840 --> 00:56.280
We build microchips from lithographic masks.

00:56.280 --> 01:02.440
So naturally, for the last several decades, we just assumed biology was a highly advanced manufacturing.

01:02.700 --> 01:03.580
Yeah, that makes sense.

01:03.880 --> 01:05.920
When the Human Genome Project kicked off,

01:06.160 --> 01:09.740
the rhetoric was all about decoding the instruction manual of life.

01:10.180 --> 01:15.400
You know, the general assumption was that DNA acted as this rigid dictatorial master plan

01:15.400 --> 01:20.820
where every anatomical outcome was explicitly coded and centrally governed.

01:20.820 --> 01:25.040
The problem is when you step out of the metaphor and look at the actual physics of neurodevelopment

01:25.040 --> 01:32.720
and really the physics of how any highly complex system forms that blueprint model completely crumbles.

01:32.920 --> 01:34.460
I mean, the math simply does not work.

01:34.580 --> 01:36.420
No, it doesn't. Not even close.

01:36.540 --> 01:41.600
You cannot compress the spatial coordinates and wiring diagrams for 16 billion neurons

01:41.600 --> 01:46.940
into a genome that only contains, what, roughly 20,000 protein-coding genes.

01:47.080 --> 01:47.940
Yeah, around 20,000.

01:48.020 --> 01:51.240
And just for context, a fruit fly has about 14,000 genes.

01:51.400 --> 01:51.560
Right.

01:51.820 --> 01:55.380
So we aren't working with a blueprint that is exponentially larger than a fly's.

01:55.620 --> 01:59.380
We are working with a system that somehow generates massive precise complexity

01:59.380 --> 02:01.700
without a master plan at all.

02:02.020 --> 02:07.180
So we're looking at a reality that is far more mysterious and, frankly, far more elegant.

02:07.600 --> 02:09.600
Which forces a total paradigm shift.

02:09.600 --> 02:13.800
If nature isn't printing out a blueprint, what is the actual mechanism?

02:14.080 --> 02:18.920
I mean, how does a biological system reliably construct something as delicate as a human brain

02:18.920 --> 02:23.040
or even just a functional leaf from a single fertilized cell

02:23.040 --> 02:25.940
without a central processor telling every molecule where to go?

02:26.300 --> 02:27.100
Okay, let's unpack this.

02:27.240 --> 02:30.120
Because answering that question is the mission of this deep dive.

02:30.480 --> 02:34.580
Today, we are taking a massive stack of highly interdisciplinary papers

02:34.580 --> 02:39.060
published between 2025 and 2026 by the Theoretical Research Flyxion

02:39.060 --> 02:46.180
and we are going to use this research to completely dismantle the blueprint model of reality.

02:46.540 --> 02:48.500
It's a huge undertaking, but it's so fascinating.

02:48.700 --> 02:49.340
It really is.

02:49.580 --> 02:52.020
We are going to guide you through a radical new framework

02:52.020 --> 02:55.680
for understanding how structure and intelligence actually arise.

02:56.080 --> 02:58.640
We're going to see that the universe doesn't use central planning.

02:58.840 --> 03:01.880
It relies on two incredibly powerful concepts, right?

03:02.240 --> 03:05.540
Constraint geometry and self-modifying propagation media.

03:05.540 --> 03:08.200
Yeah, and I know they sound like heavy theoretical terms,

03:08.600 --> 03:13.220
but at their core, they just describe systems that build the walls around themselves as they grow.

03:13.960 --> 03:16.000
You know, instead of following a predetermined map,

03:16.300 --> 03:20.220
these systems change their own physical rules and physical boundaries as they develop.

03:20.540 --> 03:22.500
They are entirely context dependent.

03:23.020 --> 03:25.040
We have an ambitious roadmap for you today,

03:25.100 --> 03:27.780
and we are traveling across a staggering range of scales.

03:27.780 --> 03:31.640
We will start small, looking at the hidden math of houseplant leaves

03:31.640 --> 03:36.740
and how fish embryos essentially, like, turn themselves solid to survive.

03:36.880 --> 03:38.020
That part is incredible.

03:38.380 --> 03:43.480
Yeah, and then we'll move up to how the human brain evolved to process memory and context

03:43.480 --> 03:45.500
without actually rewiring itself.

03:46.240 --> 03:48.320
From there, we are jumping into theoretical physics

03:48.320 --> 03:51.700
to explore a unified theory that connects the gravity of planets

03:51.700 --> 03:54.040
directly to the semantic meaning of human ideas.

03:54.220 --> 03:55.780
Which is just a massive leap.

03:55.940 --> 03:56.460
Huge leap.

03:56.460 --> 03:59.940
And finally, we will bring it all back down to Earth and out to space.

04:00.380 --> 04:02.920
Actually, to explore why the tech industry's obsession

04:02.920 --> 04:05.940
with sending artificial intelligence data centers to the moon

04:05.940 --> 04:09.300
is a thermodynamic and cognitive disaster.

04:09.760 --> 04:11.620
It is a massive intellectual journey.

04:11.780 --> 04:14.480
I mean, we are crossing botany, evolutionary neuroscience,

04:14.780 --> 04:16.840
quantum mechanics, and AI infrastructure.

04:17.140 --> 04:20.640
But the core thread running through every single one of these disciplines

04:20.640 --> 04:23.620
is the exact same mathematical and physical truth.

04:23.620 --> 04:26.380
The sheer ambition of Flyxion's work is wild.

04:26.460 --> 04:29.860
But to destroy the blueprint model of reality,

04:29.860 --> 04:32.380
we can't really start with abstract physics.

04:32.680 --> 04:36.700
We need to look at how nature actually builds physical structures in the dirt.

04:36.700 --> 04:38.980
So, let's look at a plant.

04:39.680 --> 04:44.660
Specifically, the Pelea peperomeoids, which you might know as the Chinese money plant.

04:44.660 --> 04:46.820
Right. It is a very common house plant.

04:47.060 --> 04:50.120
It has these beautiful, round, coin-like leaves.

04:50.480 --> 04:52.540
And if you hold one of those leaves up to the light,

04:52.880 --> 04:54.720
you will see a dense network of veins.

04:54.720 --> 04:57.640
But notice that it's not a simple branching pattern.

04:57.940 --> 04:58.640
Right. It's not like a tree.

04:59.160 --> 05:03.320
Exactly. It doesn't just look like a tree trunk splitting into smaller and smaller twigs.

05:03.540 --> 05:06.940
It forms a reticulate network, a closed-loop system.

05:07.420 --> 05:11.840
The veins intersect to form these interconnected, irregular polygons all over the leaf.

05:11.840 --> 05:16.160
And explaining those polygons has been a massive headache for biologists, right?

05:17.060 --> 05:21.560
For a long time, the dominant theory was something called Sachs' canalization hypothesis.

05:21.960 --> 05:24.860
Yeah. Sachs' hypothesis was brilliant for its time.

05:25.320 --> 05:30.920
The idea was that a plant hormone called auxin acts almost like water carving a riverbed.

05:31.380 --> 05:37.360
You know, the hormone flows from a source, say, the tip of the leaf down toward the base, which is the sink.

05:37.360 --> 05:42.820
And as it flows, it physically carves out a channel, and that channel eventually becomes the vein.

05:43.340 --> 05:48.220
It perfectly explains why a plant has a main central vein and a few major branches off of it.

05:48.800 --> 05:51.700
But rivers flowing to an ocean don't carve closed circles.

05:52.380 --> 05:58.180
I mean, you don't see a river split loop around and cross back over itself to form a perfect little geometric polygon.

05:59.060 --> 06:03.180
Sachs' theory kind of breaks down when you try to explain the closed loops of the Pilea plant.

06:03.180 --> 06:09.860
To solve this, researchers named Zhang and colleagues published a paper in 2026, which Flyxion heavily analyzed.

06:10.520 --> 06:12.720
They proved the plant isn't drawing vans at all.

06:12.920 --> 06:17.340
The vans were actually mapping out a strict mathematical structure known as a Voronoi diagram.

06:17.800 --> 06:18.280
Oh, wow.

06:18.540 --> 06:22.740
Okay, so if you are familiar with a Voronoi diagram, think about a city with a bunch of pizza delivery places.

06:23.100 --> 06:26.960
If every person in the city simply orders from the pizza place closest to their house,

06:27.100 --> 06:30.020
the city naturally divides up into distinct territories, right?

06:30.020 --> 06:30.420
Exactly.

06:30.420 --> 06:37.940
The boundary line between two territories is the exact middle ground where a house is equally distant from two different pizza shops.

06:38.540 --> 06:41.800
That map of territories is a Voronoi diagram.

06:42.260 --> 06:46.220
It partitions space based on distance to specific center points.

06:46.520 --> 06:50.300
And the researchers hypothesized that the leaf was doing exactly this.

06:50.960 --> 06:57.600
A Pilea leaf has these tiny microscopic secretory pores called hydathodes scattered across its surface.

06:57.600 --> 07:04.020
The researchers wondered if the plant was using these hydathodes as the center points at the pizza shops, to use your analogy.

07:04.520 --> 07:06.880
They ran the math, and the results were just incredible.

07:07.220 --> 07:15.980
They overlaid a computer-generated Voronoi diagram using the hydathodes as the center points directly on top of the actual biological veins of the leaf.

07:16.800 --> 07:21.380
To measure how well they matched, they used a statistical tool called a Jaccard index.

07:21.380 --> 07:26.680
Right. And just so everyone knows, the score of 1.0 means it's a flawless, pixel-perfect match.

07:27.040 --> 07:29.660
A score of 0.0 means they have absolutely nothing in common.

07:29.820 --> 07:31.260
Right. So what was the score?

07:31.440 --> 07:34.860
The overlap score to a Jaccard index of Bureau 0.72.

07:35.140 --> 07:35.440
Wow.

07:35.440 --> 07:48.040
In biological systems, which are inherently messy and subject to all sorts of environmental noise, a 0.72 is a highly accurate, undeniably non-random structural match.

07:48.500 --> 07:51.340
The plant is undeniably executing Voronoi math.

07:51.600 --> 07:57.060
But to prove it, they had to pit it against the best alternative model, which is the ADD tree model.

07:57.060 --> 08:00.840
Yeah, the KDT tree model is the mathematical equivalent of the classic blueprint.

08:01.080 --> 08:02.740
It's a hierarchical, top-down model.

08:02.860 --> 08:07.460
You take a space, divide it in half, then divide those halves in half, and continue branching downwards.

08:07.740 --> 08:11.400
It perfectly mimics how human engineers design distribution networks.

08:11.400 --> 08:18.380
But when they tested the KDT tree model against the actual Pilea leaf, the Jaccard index plummeted to 0.40,

08:19.140 --> 08:24.100
which, statistically speaking, is basically indistinguishable from just throwing random lines on the leaf.

08:24.100 --> 08:27.480
The hierarchical blueprint model utterly failed.

08:27.840 --> 08:31.060
The Voronoi distance partition model won decisively.

08:31.420 --> 08:34.700
And the implications are wild when you think about the mechanism.

08:35.080 --> 08:35.200
Yeah.

08:35.340 --> 08:37.380
A plant does not have a central nervous system.

08:37.740 --> 08:41.940
It does not have a calculator to measure distances and plot geometric midpoints.

08:42.040 --> 08:43.360
Right. How's it doing the math?

08:43.500 --> 08:46.000
It uses the fluid dynamics of its own hormones.

08:46.680 --> 08:50.660
Those hydophodes, the pores, act as the sources for the auxin hormone.

08:50.660 --> 08:55.920
But instead of releasing a strong, continuous river of auxin to carve a channel,

08:56.380 --> 08:59.860
they emit very weak, slow, pulsing waves.

09:00.180 --> 09:03.920
The hormone ripples outward from every single pore simultaneously.

09:04.240 --> 09:08.040
So it's like dropping dozens of pebbles into a completely still pond all at once.

09:08.360 --> 09:12.120
The leaf isn't taking a pen and drawing the boundaries where the ripples crash into each other.

09:12.260 --> 09:14.660
It is just deciding where to drop the pebbles.

09:14.940 --> 09:17.260
The physical collision of the waves builds the walls.

09:17.260 --> 09:22.480
Where the wave from one hydophode crashes into the wave from its neighbor, the hormones pile up.

09:22.640 --> 09:23.880
They form a concentration ridge.

09:24.320 --> 09:30.380
That collision locus, that standing wave of hormones, literally solidifies and becomes the physical vein.

09:30.620 --> 09:33.480
The plant does not specify the complex vein network.

09:33.820 --> 09:36.240
It only specifies the position of the hydophodes.

09:36.580 --> 09:39.280
And the physics of wave interference does the rest.

09:39.800 --> 09:42.920
This is our first major example of partition geometry.

09:42.920 --> 09:51.100
The system organizes its space through equal spatial balance rather than trying to calculate the shortest path from point A to point B.

09:51.100 --> 09:57.320
The leaf is computing complex spatial geometry using the physical constraints of fluid dynamics.

09:57.620 --> 10:00.820
That is brilliant for a flat, two-dimensional leaf.

10:01.380 --> 10:05.660
But biology isn't just drawing static lines on a flat surface, you know.

10:05.660 --> 10:11.020
It has to build massive 3D living tissues that constantly move and change shape.

10:11.340 --> 10:14.000
How does a system without a blueprint know when to stop growing?

10:14.380 --> 10:15.400
Right, that's the big question.

10:15.560 --> 10:19.260
To figure that out, we have to scale up from a plant to an animal embryo.

10:19.740 --> 10:24.840
Specifically, a 2026 paper by Autorino and colleagues looking at the zebrafish blastoderm.

10:25.100 --> 10:30.460
Okay, so during embryonic development, a ball of identical cells has to somehow figure out how to differentiate.

10:30.460 --> 10:35.440
It has to know where to draw the boundary for a completely new tissue layer like the mesoendoderm.

10:36.000 --> 10:39.120
Classical biology relied on the morphogen gradient model for this.

10:39.220 --> 10:40.460
The old GPS model.

10:40.780 --> 10:41.220
Exactly.

10:41.860 --> 10:46.360
The idea was that a chemical signal is released at a source and diffuses outward through the tissue,

10:46.740 --> 10:49.300
slowly losing concentration the further it goes.

10:49.880 --> 10:52.560
The cells act like tiny, passive sensors.

10:53.040 --> 10:54.680
They read the concentration of that chemical.

10:55.160 --> 10:57.620
If the signal is strong, they become cell type A.

10:57.620 --> 11:00.720
If the signal is weak, they become cell type B.

11:01.060 --> 11:05.180
Which treats the developing embryo like it's just an empty jar of water.

11:05.620 --> 11:05.800
Yeah.

11:05.900 --> 11:11.740
It assumes a tissue is a completely passive backdrop that the signal just harmlessly floats through to deliver a message.

11:12.280 --> 11:19.480
But the Autorino paper proved that the zebrafish embryo does something totally different with a morphogen called nodal.

11:19.900 --> 11:24.820
Yeah, nodal is the chemical signal that instructs embryonic cells to become the mesoendodermal layer.

11:24.820 --> 11:29.120
But the researchers made a startling discovery the tissue is not a passive backdrop.

11:29.540 --> 11:35.100
As the nodal morphogen diffuses, it actually alters the physical, mechanical state of the cells it is traveling through.

11:35.440 --> 11:37.820
It triggers a phenomenon known as rigidity percolation.

11:38.800 --> 11:39.860
Rigidity percolation.

11:40.740 --> 11:43.040
So the tissue is undergoing a phase transition.

11:43.560 --> 11:47.740
It starts in a fluid-like state where the cells are loose and kind of flushing around.

11:48.160 --> 11:51.720
But as the signal hits them, they transition into a solid-like state.

11:52.440 --> 11:54.440
And there's a mathematical tipping point for this, right?

11:54.440 --> 11:56.360
There is a critical threshold.

11:56.860 --> 12:01.160
The transition occurs when the cells form roughly four physical contacts per cell.

12:02.100 --> 12:05.740
When nodal hits a cell, it drives the cell to increase its surface tension.

12:06.540 --> 12:08.580
The cells become structurally stickier.

12:09.040 --> 12:10.460
They bind together more tightly.

12:11.080 --> 12:16.500
As the cells hit that threshold of four contacts, a giant, rigid cluster forms across the tissue.

12:17.020 --> 12:19.660
The embryo physically hardens in that specific region.

12:19.660 --> 12:25.060
Wait, if the cells just get rigid and tightly packed, doesn't that block the chemical signal from moving any further?

12:25.380 --> 12:26.700
That is the entire point.

12:27.020 --> 12:31.400
As the tissue gets rigid, the tiny interstitial pores and spaces between the cells collapse.

12:31.900 --> 12:33.380
The tissue becomes impermeable.

12:33.920 --> 12:38.320
Because those pores collapse, the nodal morphogen physically cannot diffuse any further.

12:38.780 --> 12:41.620
The signal stops itself by hardening the tissue around it.

12:41.800 --> 12:43.640
It literally builds its own cage.

12:43.640 --> 12:46.740
What's fascinating here is that the tissue is an active medium.

12:47.300 --> 12:50.860
It is a perfect example of a self-modifying propagation medium.

12:51.500 --> 12:54.280
The tissue does not just let signals pass through.

12:54.560 --> 12:58.600
The signal changes the medium, which in turn alters how future signals can travel.

12:58.680 --> 13:03.460
The researchers proved this with an optogenetic rescue experiment, which honestly sounds like science fiction.

13:03.460 --> 13:10.140
They took meat and zebrafish embryos, where this specific virginity transition was genetically broken.

13:10.600 --> 13:11.940
The tissue stayed fluid.

13:12.080 --> 13:12.280
Right.

13:12.380 --> 13:13.020
It never hardened.

13:13.220 --> 13:20.780
And because the tissue never hardened, the nodal signal just kept washing through the embryo, diffusing way too far, ruining the developmental pattern.

13:21.360 --> 13:23.380
The embryo was failing to form properly.

13:23.520 --> 13:25.940
To fix it, they did not alter the chemical signal.

13:26.180 --> 13:28.880
They didn't inject more nodal or add a chemical inhibitor.

13:29.240 --> 13:30.160
They used lasers.

13:30.160 --> 13:34.740
Optogenetics allows researchers to control cell behavior using light.

13:35.160 --> 13:40.980
They fired precise lasers at the tissue, manually forcing the cells to contract and increase their surface tension.

13:41.320 --> 13:45.120
They used light to manually restore the physical rigidity of the tissue.

13:45.460 --> 13:47.880
They just physically tightened the cells, and what happened?

13:48.540 --> 13:50.260
The chemical signaling fixed itself.

13:50.900 --> 13:52.900
The boundary formed in the exact right place.

13:53.240 --> 13:57.540
They proved that the physical geometry of the tissue is the actual regulator.

13:57.540 --> 14:02.700
If you fix the physical constraints, the biological program runs perfectly.

14:03.060 --> 14:07.260
It perfectly captures Flyxion's core thesis on biological self-assembly.

14:07.700 --> 14:12.180
Biology does not organize by specifying endpoints or writing trajectories in a blueprint.

14:12.740 --> 14:19.340
It organizes by continuously modulating the constraints, what Flyxion calls the admissibility structure of the system.

14:19.340 --> 14:24.000
The fish doesn't have a genetic code that says stop the nodal signal at coordinate X.

14:24.360 --> 14:30.220
The code simply says harden the tissue as the signal passes, and the laws of physics naturally halt the signal.

14:30.340 --> 14:35.000
Okay, so we've definitively destroyed the blueprint model for leaf veins in fish embryos.

14:35.000 --> 14:40.600
But at the start of this deep dive, we promised you, the listener, that we were going to build a human neocortex.

14:41.380 --> 14:50.680
If simple physical constraints can build a leaf and an embryo, how does this exact same principle scale up to build the most complex cognitive architecture on the planet?

14:51.000 --> 14:56.240
To bridge that gap, we need to examine the evolutionary boundary between us and our mammalian relatives.

14:56.240 --> 15:04.660
A 2026 paper by Javen and Collies did a massive comparative study on the development of the human neocortex versus the mouse neocortex.

15:05.280 --> 15:11.620
The neocortex is the seat of our highest cognitive functions, language, abstract thought, spatial reasoning.

15:11.960 --> 15:17.060
And the human neocortex is massively expanded and highly folded compared to a mouse.

15:17.180 --> 15:25.800
Right. And the natural assumption for decades has been that humans must possess a secret arsenal of fancy, highly advanced, human-exclusive genes.

15:26.240 --> 15:30.020
We assume our blueprint is just thicker and more detailed than the mouse blueprint.

15:30.260 --> 15:32.500
But the genomic data completely refutes that.

15:32.760 --> 15:37.160
The molecular parts, the structural genes that physically build the neurons in the cortex, are ancient.

15:37.640 --> 15:39.460
They are highly conserved across species.

15:40.080 --> 15:44.480
The fundamental difference between a mouse brain and a human brain is not in what genes we possess,

15:44.480 --> 15:47.480
but in when and where those genes are permitted to activate.

15:47.900 --> 15:51.740
It is an evolution of accessibility, not an evolution of molecular novelty.

15:51.740 --> 15:56.440
Let's look at the specific gene Javen's team highlighted, J-U-N-B.

15:57.260 --> 16:01.160
It's a transcription factor, meaning it helps turn other genes on or off.

16:01.960 --> 16:06.340
In a developing mouse, J-U-N-B activates very late in the developmental process.

16:06.920 --> 16:14.120
It turns on in mature post-mitotic neurons, cells that have already committed to their final identity and have permanently stopped dividing.

16:14.360 --> 16:14.940
Exactly.

16:14.940 --> 16:19.160
But in a developing human, J-U-N-B turns on significantly earlier.

16:19.660 --> 16:22.340
It activates in radioglia progenitor cells.

16:22.900 --> 16:27.580
These are the active stem cells that are rapidly dividing and generating the raw material of the brain.

16:27.840 --> 16:29.700
That is a categorical shift in function.

16:30.080 --> 16:32.260
The genetic sequence of J-U-N-B is identical,

16:32.580 --> 16:36.580
but the regulatory geometry that dictates its activation is completely different.

16:37.280 --> 16:39.120
This phenomenon is called heterocrony.

16:39.800 --> 16:44.740
Heteroprony simply means changing the timing of developmental events without changing the events themselves.

16:45.060 --> 16:49.580
Wait, so are you telling me the boundary between a mouse brain and a human brain isn't a hard genetic wall,

16:49.800 --> 16:51.160
but just a timing trick?

16:51.300 --> 16:53.660
Like, we just let the biological oven on a little longer?

16:53.820 --> 16:58.020
How does just changing the timing of constraints create completely new physical structures?

16:58.260 --> 17:00.840
Think about it like changing the zoning laws in a city.

17:01.400 --> 17:06.760
You don't need to invent new types of bricks or genetically engineer new types of construction workers.

17:06.760 --> 17:11.880
If you simply delay the signal that tells the workers to stop building residential zoning,

17:12.480 --> 17:19.360
the exact same workers using the exact same bricks will build a massive skyscraper instead of a two-story house.

17:19.560 --> 17:21.080
Okay, that makes total sense.

17:21.200 --> 17:27.700
By keeping those progenitor cells dividing just a little bit longer before they receive the constraint signal to stop and differentiate,

17:27.700 --> 17:32.180
you exponentially increase the total volume of neurons produced.

17:32.580 --> 17:38.320
You generate the massive, expanded upper layers of the human cortex entirely through a timing delay.

17:38.580 --> 17:43.220
So the underlying developmental manifold, the total universe of possible cellular states,

17:43.380 --> 17:45.780
is totally shared between the mouse and the human.

17:46.280 --> 17:52.020
The mouse actually has the latent dormant machinery to build a human-like brain sitting right there inside its genome.

17:52.020 --> 17:54.360
It is poised, but inaccessible.

17:55.100 --> 17:58.400
The mouse's constraint geometry prevents it from going down that path.

17:59.000 --> 18:00.980
But Javed's team proved this experimentally.

18:01.400 --> 18:07.620
They took developing mouse brain tissue, and they activated an upstream regulatory gene called IRF1.

18:08.180 --> 18:10.840
IRF1 acts as a key that unlocks genetic accessibility.

18:11.500 --> 18:18.620
By artificially turning on IRF1 in the mouse, they forced the JMB gene to activate early in the progenitor cells,

18:18.980 --> 18:21.020
mimicking the human developmental timeline.

18:21.020 --> 18:23.860
And the mouse tissue started behaving like human tissue.

18:24.500 --> 18:27.940
The progenitor cells stayed active longer, they produced more neurons,

18:28.220 --> 18:32.120
