19:44.380 --> 19:47.200
But physical anatomy is just the static highway system.

19:47.200 --> 19:53.180
Knowing the layout of the roads doesn't tell you where the traffic is actually going or what the drivers are thinking.

19:53.900 --> 19:58.800
Gonzalez's team investigated how information actually flows from the hippocampus,

19:59.220 --> 20:01.800
specifically the CA3 and CA1 regions,

20:02.020 --> 20:08.840
which handle fast, highly plastic learning and immediate spatial memory out to the retrosplenial cortex, or RSC,

20:09.460 --> 20:12.960
which is responsible for stable, long-term spatial representations.

20:12.960 --> 20:17.720
And they found that communication isn't just sending a pulse of electricity down a rigid wire.

20:18.140 --> 20:22.320
It relies on a mathematical phenomenon called communication subspaces.

20:22.760 --> 20:25.780
Yeah, imagine a massive crowd of people standing in a stadium.

20:26.240 --> 20:31.480
They're all physically present in the same space, but they can organize themselves into countless different patterns.

20:31.600 --> 20:36.120
They could form a wave, they could split into geometric shapes, they could group by the colors of their shirts.

20:36.120 --> 20:42.540
A subspace in the brain is a specific mathematical pattern of coordinated firing among a population of neurons.

20:42.900 --> 20:47.160
What Gonzalez's team discovered is a mechanism called subspace rotation.

20:47.660 --> 20:50.360
To put this in perspective for you, the person listening to this right now,

20:50.780 --> 20:54.180
when you walk into your kitchen and instantly remember you need to buy milk,

20:54.640 --> 20:59.860
your brain isn't growing new wires to connect the concept of the kitchen to the concept of milk.

21:00.200 --> 21:02.720
Your brain is utilizing subspace rotation.

21:02.720 --> 21:10.880
Exactly. The CA1 region sits squarely between the fast-learning CA3 and the long-term storage of the RSC.

21:11.540 --> 21:18.320
It uses the exact same physical group of neurons to receive the chaotic, noisy information from CA3

21:18.320 --> 21:21.340
and to transmit the refined information out to the RSC.

21:21.860 --> 21:24.920
But it doesn't just pass the message straight through like a game of telephone.

21:25.300 --> 21:30.860
It organizes the firing patterns of those identical neurons into completely different mathematical subspaces.

21:30.860 --> 21:35.100
And not just slightly different. The inputs and outputs are nearly orthogonal.

21:35.760 --> 21:41.720
If you map the input subspace from CA3 and the output subspace to the RSC as geometric angles,

21:42.080 --> 21:47.340
they're almost 90 degrees apart. They're mathematically perpendicular to each other within the network.

21:47.520 --> 21:49.520
The reason the brain does this is profound.

21:49.520 --> 21:54.960
If the communication subspaces were aligned, if the signal just passed straight through,

21:55.780 --> 22:01.440
then any random noise, any fleeting, unstable thought occurring in the highly plastic CA3 region

22:01.440 --> 22:07.480
